\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Econometrics I: Quiz 3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a simple linear regression model, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=\alpha+\beta x_{t}+u_{t}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a fixed variate (strongly exogenous), and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 is i.i.d.
 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset

 variate.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be the OLSE, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\hat{\beta}}^{2}$
\end_inset

 be the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{\hat{\beta}}^{2}$
\end_inset

 be the estimated variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 You wish to test the null hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\beta=1$
\end_inset

 against the one-sided alternative hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}:\beta=1$
\end_inset

.
 The test involves a few basic elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
You need to choose a test statistic.
 Since the test is about the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, it seems appropriate to use a test statistic based on its estimate.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the name of the test based on this idea?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Specify a statistic that you can use for the test and show how to derive
 it.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Nested hypothesis test.
 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\mu=a$
\end_inset

 versus 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}:\mu>a$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\mu\leq a$
\end_inset

.
 To test the hypothesis, I would use the Wald type test.
 Fact: 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{\alpha}(1,N-K)=t_{\nicefrac{\alpha}{2}}^{2}(N-K)$
\end_inset

.
 For this test, I can use the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-statistic
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t=\frac{\hat{\beta}-1}{\sqrt{\hat{\sigma}_{\hat{\beta}}^{2}}}
\]

\end_inset

and here is how to derive it
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
t & = & \frac{\nicefrac{\left(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta\right)}{sd(R\hat{\beta})}}{\sqrt{\frac{(N-K)\hat{\sigma}^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}\cdot\frac{1}{(N-K)}}}\\
 & = & \frac{\nicefrac{\left(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta\right)}{sd(R\hat{\beta})}}{\sqrt{\nicefrac{\hat{\sigma}^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}}}\\
 & = & \frac{\left(R\hat{\beta}-R\beta\right)}{est.\ sd(R\hat{\beta})}\sim t(N-K)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
You now have to select a decision rule that prescribes the rejection region,
 i.e., when to reject and when not to reject the null hypothesis.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Specify your decision rule, including your reasonable choice of critical
 value(s) (notice that this question is asked before the following question
 on the significance level, i.e., I am asking you to specify a decision rule
 without considering the significance level at this stage).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Justify why your decision rule makes sense for the case under consideration.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
For a chosen critical value 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

, I reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $t>c$
\end_inset

.
 Under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, if the probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $t>c$
\end_inset

 is fairly low, then I will reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Any reasonable decision rule is bound to make errors in drawing conclusion.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the Type I error? What is the name of probability of Type I error?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Why can't we avoid the Type I error?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Describe how you find the probability of Type I error of your decision rule?
 (As a part of your answer, you need to talk about what distribution function
 you use).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Type I error occurs when reject a true 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 The name of probability of Type I error is known as the significance level.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is a random variable, then there will always be some probability that we
 reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 if it's true.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 changes depending on the sample, so we could obtain a 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 that is significantly different from 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=1$
\end_inset

, even if 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=1$
\end_inset

 is true.
 One way to find the probability of Type I error is 
\begin_inset Formula $P(t>c|H_{0})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is distributed as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\sim t(N-K)$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Instead of picking a specific decision rule and then compute the probability
 of Type I error, the traditional approach is to choose the probability
 of Type I error and then determine the specific decision rule.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
When would you choose a small probability of Type I error? Give an example.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Describe how you determine the critical value(s) for a given probability
 of Type I error.
 (As a part of your answer, you need to talk about what distribution function
 you use).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
I would choose a small probability of Type I error when I want to reject
 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 less frequently.
 Given a signficance level 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(t>t_{\alpha}|H_{0})=\alpha$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 is the critical value for the one-sided test.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $t\sim t(N-K)$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 We reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $t>t_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

